October 4th is national taco day tacos  are small cheap and easy to assemble and  they're delicious they're the perfect  food to get from a food truck  who doesn't love a taco truck well  restaurants and regulators that's who as  food trucks have exploded in popularity  over the past decade they've encountered  regulatory roadblocks in dozens of  cities some regulations are sensible  rules about food safety some cities have  barred food trucks from parking or  consign them to hidden corners of the  city others have banned them outright  these regulations have nothing to do  with protecting consumers and everything  to do with crushing competition in DC  Chicago El Paso and elsewhere the  restaurant lobby has fought to curb or  restrict food trucks the lobbyists  sometimes admitted their  anti-competitive intentions but often  they tossed out bogus arguments about  sidewalks safety or the like this is a  hidden aspect of regulations always  pushed in the name of protecting the  public they often serve most to protect  special interests from competition do  you think food trucks have an unfair  advantage over restaurants let us know  in our poll also let us know what other  topics you'd like our scholars to cover  in 60 seconds and be sure to LIKE and  subscribe for more research and more  videos from a EEI
 

